WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.426 --> 00:00:02.336
- [Narrator] Our journey
takes us to Costa Rica,

00:00:02.336 --> 00:00:04.813
more specifically, Tortuguero,

00:00:04.813 --> 00:00:07.275
to witness the yearly
hatching of baby sea turtles,

00:00:07.275 --> 00:00:09.737
and meet the people protecting them.

00:00:09.737 --> 00:00:11.853
We arrive by way of La Pavona,

00:00:11.853 --> 00:00:16.074
a small port town with one
restaurant and a pay toilet.

00:00:16.074 --> 00:00:17.898
Our boat operator, Jairo Guzman,

00:00:17.898 --> 00:00:19.894
takes us down the Lucky River.

00:00:19.894 --> 00:00:22.413
It is a one hour journey
to the island itself.

00:00:22.413 --> 00:00:24.246
The view is beautiful.

00:00:30.681 --> 00:00:32.948
Nearly all species of sea
turtles are endangered,

00:00:32.948 --> 00:00:34.811
and this island is a
prominent nesting ground

00:00:34.811 --> 00:00:37.915
for both green and
leatherback sea turtles.

00:00:37.915 --> 00:00:40.348
It was here in 1959 the very
first Sea Turtle Conservancy

00:00:40.348 --> 00:00:42.929
was opened by Doctor Archie Carr,

00:00:42.929 --> 00:00:44.772
to specifically study green sea turtles,

00:00:44.772 --> 00:00:47.080
as they were nearly extinct.

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:51.208
This was their original
base, this is their new base.

00:00:51.208 --> 00:00:53.010
Along with us from the Conservancy,

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.292
is Jaime, head field director.

00:00:55.292 --> 00:00:56.963
David, station manager.

00:00:56.963 --> 00:01:00.936
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:00.936 --> 00:01:03.494
Silvia, outreach coordinator,

00:01:03.494 --> 00:01:05.593
and their 10 research assistants.

00:01:05.593 --> 00:01:08.468
They all work here to educate
the public and gather data

00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:11.635
on the current sea turtle populations.

00:01:12.756 --> 00:01:15.226
On day one, we meet in
the middle of the night

00:01:15.226 --> 00:01:18.533
to count nesting turtles
and the eggs they lay.

00:01:18.533 --> 00:01:20.005
- Now let's go to the beach.

00:01:20.005 --> 00:01:21.971
- [Narrator] Around
dawn, we spot a turtle.

00:01:21.971 --> 00:01:24.560
She is laying her eggs.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:27.496
She is one of 36,000
turtles nesting this season

00:01:27.496 --> 00:01:28.944
on the beach.

00:01:28.944 --> 00:01:31.656
Each turtle will lay around 110 eggs.

00:01:31.656 --> 00:01:34.717
In two months, they will hatch.

00:01:34.717 --> 00:01:36.416
We get some sleep, and that afternoon,

00:01:36.416 --> 00:01:38.389
we ask Jaime about the hatchings.

00:01:38.389 --> 00:01:39.372
- [Woman] Take one.

00:01:39.372 --> 00:01:40.376
- [Narrator] He says--

00:01:40.376 --> 00:01:42.702
- The best time to catch
it is right at dawn,

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:45.341
around 5:30 in the morning.

00:01:45.341 --> 00:01:50.188
- [Narrator] That means we have
some downtime on the island.

00:01:50.188 --> 00:01:52.285
David invites us to his makeshift office.

00:01:52.285 --> 00:01:55.023
We discuss the vision of the Conservancy.

00:01:55.023 --> 00:01:56.521
- Basically, we're
working here on the legacy

00:01:56.521 --> 00:01:57.962
of Doctor Archie Carr.

00:01:57.962 --> 00:02:01.044
He had this idea of changing
the mentality of the people,

00:02:01.044 --> 00:02:04.714
from harvesting the turtles
for their own consumption,

00:02:04.714 --> 00:02:08.803
and for making money out
of the meat and the eggs,

00:02:08.803 --> 00:02:11.636
into an eco-tourism based culture.

00:02:12.521 --> 00:02:15.963
It's a very complex thing,
to protect the turtles.

00:02:15.963 --> 00:02:18.487
- [Narrator] On day
two, the alarm goes off,

00:02:18.487 --> 00:02:22.166
the team gathers, and
we head to the beach.

00:02:22.166 --> 00:02:25.043
- We are gonna need a bigger boat.

00:02:25.043 --> 00:02:29.487
(upbeat music)
(boat engine rumbling)

00:02:29.487 --> 00:02:32.374
- [Narrator] For a while, we see nothing.

00:02:32.374 --> 00:02:35.325
And then, baby green sea
turtles begin making their way

00:02:35.325 --> 00:02:36.408
to the ocean.

00:02:41.559 --> 00:02:43.411
Only 80% will make it out of the nest,

00:02:43.411 --> 00:02:47.547
and of those, one in 1,000
make it to adulthood.

00:02:47.547 --> 00:02:50.294
This means that out of nearly
four million hatchlings

00:02:50.294 --> 00:02:54.199
this season, only around
3,000 will become adults.

00:02:54.199 --> 00:02:56.983
And the odds are stacked against them.

00:02:56.983 --> 00:02:59.229
(waves crashing)

00:02:59.229 --> 00:03:00.996
There is more downtime.

00:03:00.996 --> 00:03:04.163
(footsteps squishing)

00:03:08.152 --> 00:03:10.153
On our final day, day three,

00:03:10.153 --> 00:03:13.876
we go to the beach to
excavate a hatched nest.

00:03:13.876 --> 00:03:15.805
The research assistants do this job.

00:03:15.805 --> 00:03:16.805
It is messy.

00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:20.742
They're counting how
many eggs have hatched.

00:03:20.742 --> 00:03:23.731
In this nest, nine did not make it.

00:03:23.731 --> 00:03:25.664
They'll add this
information to their decades

00:03:25.664 --> 00:03:28.024
of existing research.

00:03:28.024 --> 00:03:32.191
That afternoon, Jairo's father,
Guzman Senior, picks us up.

00:03:33.612 --> 00:03:36.177
As we leave Tortuguero,
I can't help but think

00:03:36.177 --> 00:03:39.234
of something Silvia said back at the base.

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:43.151
(speaking in foreign language)

00:03:53.545 --> 00:03:56.425
Sea turtles are not gonna stop
being endangered this year.

00:03:56.425 --> 00:03:58.115
And they probably won't
stop being endangered

00:03:58.115 --> 00:03:59.897
next year either.

00:03:59.897 --> 00:04:01.418
But it's good to know that
there are people out there

00:04:01.418 --> 00:04:04.451
working hard to make change happen.

00:04:04.451 --> 00:04:06.868
(soft music)

